{
  "id": 445088279,
  "iid": 23129,
  "project_id": 7898047,
  "title": "Download winbisonflex as an external artifact in CMAKE directly",
  "description": "This PR adds the winbisonflex package as an external artifact in the CMakeFile, to have it downloaded automatically instead of having to use chocolatey. This was the last remaining extra lib that was not automatically installed (to build wireshark. I'm not talking about doc, etc.), meaning there's now no need to use chocolatey (and the instructions can be simplified a lot). If you take a look at the chocolatey package, it just downloads and unzip the file: https://community.chocolatey.org/packages/winflexbison3#files , so it's pretty straightfoward.\n\n(Also, this now updates to Bison 3.8. The chocolatey package was stuck on Bison 3.7)\n\n**Side note:**\n\nI think that https://www.wireshark.org/docs/wsdg_html_chunked/ChSetupWindows.html could be decluttered a bit, and reordered. Typically I would move the steps to install dependencies for building the documentation to a separate section, make some steps shorter, etc. If you ask me, there's often a bit too much text that's not really useful. Some sections, most notably the usage of chocolatey, can probably be squashed or removed. The new version of the native Visual Studio installer/updater is also pretty good nowadays, and installs and updates cmake on its own, so that could also be greatly simplified.\n\nWith this PR, the installation process could basically be simplified down to:\n- install Visual Studio with the default C++ thingy (which includes cmake, the doc can be updated for 2026 btw, it works fine :D)\n- install git, python and Qt (using aqt for the latter. For the former, most people already have it, plus python for Windows will soon enforce to use their own installer/updater, I don't think that it's useful to use chocolatey)\n- add Qt and the wireshark folder to your global environment path\n- run cmake\n- build wireshark\n\nIf it's fine to you, I can make a PR that reorders the page (but mostly simplifies it).",
  "state": "merged",
